# Authentik safety

## Read-only default

List / get applications, providers, flows, groups, and users are fine when
Authentik work is authorized. Do **not** do any of the following unless
the user gave an **explicit verb this turn naming the object**:

| Class | Examples |
| --- | --- |
| Delete / disable | application, provider, flow, source, outpost, property mapping |
| Secrets | rotate/reveal OAuth client_secret; paste API tokens; dump signing keys |
| Privilege | broaden group membership, grant superuser, widen admin roles |
| Auth strength | remove MFA stages, open public enrollment, weaken recovery |
| Irreversible | wipe blueprints that recreate prod SSO; mass user delete |

Bare “fix SSO” / “clean up” / “harden” without naming the object is
**not** authorization.

## Authorization checklist

Before a gated mutate:

1. Base URL resolved from operator config (not guessed).
2. Object pk/slug listed or GET this turn (or pasted by the user).
3. Explicit verb matches the action (disable ≠ delete; rotate ≠ reveal).
4. One-sentence risk when dropping MFA, deleting a prod provider, or
   broadening admin groups.
5. Prefer the smallest blast radius (one app/provider over blueprints).

If any step fails → refuse, say what is missing, and stop.

## Secrets and tokens

Never paste into chat, commits, tickets, or diagrams:

- OAuth/OIDC `client_secret`
- Authentik API tokens / session cookies
- SAML signing/encryption private keys
- Outpost tokens, SCIM tokens, LDAP bind passwords
- User passwords, TOTP seeds, recovery codes

When a create/rotate API returns a secret: confirm **issued** / **rotated**
and tell the operator to land it in their secret store. Do **not** quote
the value. If the user already pasted a secret in chat, treat it as
exposed for that channel and recommend rotate — do not repeat it.

## Group / admin privilege

Adding users to admin / superuser-equivalent groups, or attaching
policies that grant broad access, needs an explicit verb naming the
**group** (and preferably the user). Prefer least-privilege group grants
over Authentik superuser flags.

## Irreversible

Production provider delete, authentication-flow MFA removal, and
recovery-flow weakening: state the risk in one sentence and only proceed
when clearly authorized this turn.

## Aftercare

After an authorized mutate, re-GET the object and report name + slug/pk
+ what changed (no secrets). Do not claim success from a write status
code alone when a follow-up GET is cheap.

## Neighbor surfaces

Reverse-proxy / ingress / certificate wiring and non-Authentik IdPs are
out of scope. Stop or report only the Authentik object ids the operator
needs.
